WEBVTT

00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:06.059
An invoice pipeline asked the model to do six things in one call.

00:00:06.359 --> 00:00:11.901
Extract the line items, add them up, apply the discount, compute the sales tax on

00:00:11.901 --> 00:00:16.143
each line, convert the date, and validate the issuer's tax ID.

00:00:16.793 --> 00:00:19.390
On most invoices, the total matched.

00:00:19.690 --> 00:00:24.752
But in one batch, three invoices in a hundred reached the accounting system with the

00:00:24.752 --> 00:00:27.041
tax wrong in the second decimal place.

00:00:27.691 --> 00:00:28.920
And there was no pattern.

00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:31.343
Sometimes it was rounding.

00:00:31.993 --> 00:00:35.132
Sometimes a line counted twice in the sum.

00:00:35.782 --> 00:00:41.404
And sometimes a digit of the tax ID that the model decided to fix, all on its own.

00:00:42.054 --> 00:00:47.539
Arithmetic, sales tax and check digits aren't a matter of interpretation.

00:00:47.839 --> 00:00:49.024
They're rules.

00:00:49.324 --> 00:00:52.721
The model generates the next token by probability.

00:00:53.021 --> 00:00:54.664
A sum function doesn't.

00:00:54.964 --> 00:01:00.179
In this lesson, you'll move that work out of the model, safely, and discover a bonus

00:01:00.179 --> 00:01:01.855
you probably didn't expect.

00:01:03.055 --> 00:01:07.774
The solution is to take the deterministic operation out of the prompt.

00:01:08.074 --> 00:01:12.102
The model extracts, or decides, what only it can.

00:01:12.402 --> 00:01:17.284
A regular function does the math, the formatting, or the validation.

00:01:17.584 --> 00:01:22.324
And when the model needs the result in the middle of a conversation, that function

00:01:22.324 --> 00:01:23.884
is offered to it as a tool.

00:01:25.084 --> 00:01:29.240
Before, understanding and calculating happen in the same call.

00:01:29.540 --> 00:01:34.517
The invoice goes in, numbers come out, and they go straight into the system.

00:01:35.167 --> 00:01:39.309
After, the model reads, and code calculates and checks.

00:01:39.609 --> 00:01:45.684
The invoice arrives, and the model only extracts values, exactly as printed.

00:01:46.334 --> 00:01:51.764
Regular functions handle the sums, the tax, the dates and the tax ID check.

00:01:52.414 --> 00:01:56.006
Code compares the calculated total with the printed total.

00:01:56.656 --> 00:02:00.028
And only a coherent invoice goes into the system.

00:02:00.328 --> 00:02:02.168
A mismatch goes to review.

00:02:03.368 --> 00:02:04.741
Here's one invoice.

00:02:05.041 --> 00:02:07.429
The model returns two items.

00:02:07.729 --> 00:02:12.452
Two units at one hundred fifty dollars, and one unit at four hundred.

00:02:12.752 --> 00:02:15.051
Both with an eighteen percent tax rate.

00:02:15.351 --> 00:02:16.776
No discount.

00:02:17.076 --> 00:02:20.152
And a printed total of seven hundred dollars.

00:02:20.802 --> 00:02:21.972
Code does the math.

00:02:22.272 --> 00:02:26.577
Two times one fifty, plus four hundred, is seven hundred.

00:02:26.877 --> 00:02:28.729
It matches the printed total.

00:02:29.029 --> 00:02:34.867
Then it computes the tax with the rounding rule the tax team chose, and validates

00:02:34.867 --> 00:02:35.660
the tax ID.

00:02:36.310 --> 00:02:37.721
Now here's the bonus.

00:02:38.021 --> 00:02:41.240
Say the model had misread one fifty as fifteen.

00:02:41.540 --> 00:02:45.370
The sum wouldn't reach seven hundred, and the invoice would go to review.

00:02:46.020 --> 00:02:51.148
Before, the model could quietly adjust the sum to make it close, and the reading

00:02:51.148 --> 00:02:52.623
error just disappeared.

00:02:52.923 --> 00:02:57.291
Now the math in code becomes a checker for the extraction.

00:02:57.591 --> 00:03:01.406
The error that used to slip by silently now raises a flag.

00:03:02.606 --> 00:03:04.026
Here's one for you.

00:03:04.326 --> 00:03:07.375
The team created a tool called sum items.

00:03:07.675 --> 00:03:11.153
It sends the amounts to a small model, and asks for the sum.

00:03:11.453 --> 00:03:13.546
The math eval improved a little.

00:03:13.846 --> 00:03:15.584
Is the refactoring done?

00:03:18.634 --> 00:03:19.378
No.

00:03:19.678 --> 00:03:22.370
The math left one prompt and entered another.

00:03:22.670 --> 00:03:27.992
It's still token generation, it still makes mistakes, and now the mistake lives in a

00:03:27.992 --> 00:03:29.702
tool with an innocent name.

00:03:30.352 --> 00:03:32.467
A tool, here, is a function.

00:03:32.767 --> 00:03:36.154
Sum, a rounding function, a tax ID validator.

00:03:36.454 --> 00:03:37.565
Here's the test.

00:03:37.865 --> 00:03:43.485
If the operation can't be unit tested without calling a model's API, it hasn't left

00:03:43.485 --> 00:03:44.432
the model yet.

00:03:44.732 --> 00:03:47.683
And cheaper and still wrong is still wrong.

00:03:48.883 --> 00:03:50.431
Now the mechanics.

00:03:51.081 --> 00:03:52.847
Step zero, measure.

00:03:53.147 --> 00:03:56.453
Run the extraction eval, and split the errors by type.

00:03:56.753 --> 00:03:59.920
Reading errors, where a value was misread.

00:04:00.220 --> 00:04:04.379
And math errors, in the sum, the tax or the format.

00:04:04.679 --> 00:04:09.214
If you don't split them, you'll think the model improved, when it only got the sum

00:04:09.214 --> 00:04:10.376
right by coincidence.

00:04:11.026 --> 00:04:14.167
List the deterministic operations in the prompt.

00:04:14.467 --> 00:04:18.922
Every verb of calculation, conversion or validation.

00:04:19.222 --> 00:04:22.791
Add up, compute, convert, validate the check digit.

00:04:23.441 --> 00:04:28.566
Write a function for each one, with unit tests, including the edge cases.

00:04:28.866 --> 00:04:35.476
Half a cent, a zero rate, national holidays, a tax ID whose check digit is zero.

00:04:35.776 --> 00:04:40.943
And the tax rule comes from a conversation with whoever owns tax, not from the

00:04:40.943 --> 00:04:41.870
model's guess.

00:04:42.520 --> 00:04:47.259
Rewrite the prompt to ask only for raw data, as it appears in the document.

00:04:47.559 --> 00:04:52.921
And take the computed fields, like the tax and the total, out of what the model

00:04:52.921 --> 00:04:53.532
fills in.

00:04:54.182 --> 00:04:57.218
Wire the functions in, right after extraction.

00:04:57.868 --> 00:05:03.340
Or, if the model needs the result during a conversation, expose them as tools.

00:05:03.640 --> 00:05:08.541
A delivery assistant that needs the next business day mid-conversation calls a

00:05:08.541 --> 00:05:10.614
function that adds business days.

00:05:10.914 --> 00:05:12.649
The model doesn't add days.

00:05:12.949 --> 00:05:13.934
It asks.

00:05:14.584 --> 00:05:16.579
Use mismatches as an alarm.

00:05:16.879 --> 00:05:21.030
When the code's math disagrees with the document, send it to review.

00:05:21.680 --> 00:05:22.830
And measure again.

00:05:23.130 --> 00:05:25.415
Math errors should drop to zero.

00:05:25.715 --> 00:05:29.465
Reading errors are still there, but now the cross-check catches them.

00:05:30.665 --> 00:05:31.676
A few risks.

00:05:31.976 --> 00:05:34.443
Interpretation hiding inside the math.

00:05:34.743 --> 00:05:37.429
Total may or may not include shipping.

00:05:37.729 --> 00:05:42.355
Settle that rule with someone who knows the business, before you code it.

00:05:42.655 --> 00:05:45.878
Otherwise you swap the model's error for yours.

00:05:46.178 --> 00:05:49.264
Deterministic, and on every single invoice.

00:05:49.914 --> 00:05:51.280
Raw formats vary.

00:05:51.580 --> 00:05:55.173
Asking for a date as written pushes the parsing into code.

00:05:55.473 --> 00:05:59.005
Test the parser on real formats from the invoice folder.

00:05:59.655 --> 00:06:01.883
A tool the model ignores.

00:06:02.183 --> 00:06:07.091
If the function is a tool, the model may skip it and calculate on its own.

00:06:07.391 --> 00:06:11.945
Say in the description when to use it, and check the trace for the tool call.

00:06:12.595 --> 00:06:14.328
And more reviews, at first.

00:06:14.628 --> 00:06:17.994
The cross-check will catch errors that used to pass silently.

00:06:18.294 --> 00:06:19.250
That's the goal.

00:06:19.550 --> 00:06:23.617
But warn the operations team before they think something broke.

00:06:24.267 --> 00:06:28.576
And if operations does complain, don't switch the alarm off.

00:06:28.876 --> 00:06:31.570
The old normal volume was invisible error.

00:06:31.870 --> 00:06:33.738
Classify the reviews.

00:06:34.038 --> 00:06:38.268
Separate real mismatches from a business rule you coded badly.

00:06:38.568 --> 00:06:39.876
And fix the right one.

00:06:40.526 --> 00:06:43.123
There's one tempting shortcut to resist.

00:06:43.423 --> 00:06:48.208
The code's sum and the printed total differ by two percent, and someone suggests

00:06:48.208 --> 00:06:52.873
letting the model adjust the total, because it understands implicit discounts.

00:06:53.173 --> 00:06:54.762
That's the smell coming back.

00:06:55.062 --> 00:06:59.692
A real discount becomes an extracted field, not a silent adjustment.

00:07:00.892 --> 00:07:02.888
So what stays with the model?

00:07:03.188 --> 00:07:06.552
Anything with no closed rule a function could apply.

00:07:06.852 --> 00:07:12.415
Deciding whether a contract paragraph is a penalty clause or a confidentiality

00:07:12.415 --> 00:07:14.198
clause is classification.

00:07:14.498 --> 00:07:17.696
No function computes the meaning of a paragraph.

00:07:17.996 --> 00:07:22.946
Validating a check digit, or turning a date into a calendar date, is code.

00:07:24.146 --> 00:07:26.131
So here's the idea to take with you.

00:07:26.431 --> 00:07:28.714
Split reading from calculating.

00:07:29.014 --> 00:07:33.771
The model copies what's on the page, functions do every new number, and the

00:07:33.771 --> 00:07:37.830
comparison between the two becomes your best alarm for misreads.

00:07:38.130 --> 00:07:42.850
A refactoring that removes errors, and also finds the ones you couldn't see.

00:07:43.500 --> 00:07:47.226
So far, the errors came from the model reading wrong.

00:07:47.526 --> 00:07:52.817
In the next lesson, we'll look at what happens when the call itself fails, and the

00:07:52.817 --> 00:07:55.075
code just tries again: blind retry.

00:07:55.375 --> 00:07:56.205
See you there.
